  "slug": "gene-models-b8d8",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published tool pair neural sequence models with interpretability or explicit cross-species controls.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is neural sequence modeling in which interpretability or generalization is evaluated against explicit controls. EnhancerMatcher pairs a CNN with explainable AI to classify cell-type-specific enhancer activity relative to two reference enhancers, trained on CATlas data. WaveDNA recasts DNA as wavelet-transformed 2D images so pretrained CNNs can be reused for TF binding site prediction, with interpretability and speed as stated aims. A cross-species study finds that CNNs trained on liver chromatin accessibility generalize within and across five mammals yet fail to predict quantitative accessibility differences, a clear boundary on current models. A separate preprint shows that compact two-hidden-layer feed-forward networks with sliding-window context predict protein residue-RNA base binding, with ReLU, GLU, and Tanh variants converging. What is new is the emphasis on compact architectures and on representation changes that reuse existing tools, rather than scale alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-15"
}
